// flyover -- a hardware-accelerated terrain flyover simulator in `id`. It opens
// a window via the native GL backend (host/dev/win/gl), generates a rolling-hills
// heightmap with an integer Park-Miller PRNG + a few smoothing passes (no
// floats, no trig), builds one static triangle mesh from it, and then flies a
// camera forward over the landscape forever (looping once it reaches the far
// edge). See world/ for terrain generation + the frame loop's per-tick camera
// math, and loop/ for the frame loop itself.
//
//   idc/bin/idc demos/flyover --allow-untested -o flyover && ./flyover
//
// Close the window (or press Esc / q) to quit. GFX_MAX_FRAMES lets a headless
// build run N frames and exit on its own (see host/dev/win/gl).

// main()'s no-display path: the build harness runs every case with IDSTD_GFX=none
// (docs/TESTS.md), which makes glwin_open() fail the same way on every machine,
// whatever its own DISPLAY is, so ok=0
// and loop(ok) returns through spin's run<=0 branch without ever calling
// frame -- the one path that does not depend on GFX_MAX_FRAMES, an
// environment variable this build does not export.
main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  int ok = glwin_open(900, 560, "id flyover -- terrain flight");
  world_initialize();
  loop(ok);
} return int 0;
(0, []):(0) then heights_state:("614,520")
